#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119440 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/id #uuid "f0213e01-520f-5b59-96d3-1d94a50deb82" :question/math-academy-id "e-12591" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle t, -2t, t^2 \\rangle$ at the point where $t = 1.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t),$ $\\mathbf r'(1),$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(1)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,\\,-2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨1,-2,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9} \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find $\\mathbf r''(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-2),2\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,i-2\\,j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{20} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{{3}^{3}}=\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119441 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "b17659af-7eb8-5922-a8b7-ab1e9dcfda08" :question/math-academy-id "e-12592" :question/problem "Using the cross product formula for curvature, find the curvature of $\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle 2t, 1+t^2\\rangle$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the curvature $\\kappa (t),$ we will use the following formula:\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3}\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+{t}^{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+(2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩=⟨0,2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\mathbf r''(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2,\\,2t,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2t & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2t \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\| = 4,\n$$\n\nand therefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{{(2\\sqrt{1+{t}^{2}})}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{2{(1+{t}^{2})}^{3/2}}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119442 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/id #uuid "0ad622cf-852f-5795-81ec-d147f050074e" :question/math-academy-id "e-12608" :question/problem "Given the curve $\\mathbf r(t)=\\left\\langle t^2, \\: 2t, \\: 1 \\right\\rangle$, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $\\kappa (t)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\kappa(t) = \\dfrac{\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\times \\mathbf r''(t) \\|}{\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|^3} .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,2,\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(2)^{2}+{0}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4({t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative $\\mathbf{r}''(t)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩=⟨2,\\,0,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,2,0⟩\\times ⟨2,0,0⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2t & 2 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{4}{8({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}=\\frac{1}{2({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358838 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6d90cfb2-b731-5eae-b023-971f0457ace7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358840} {:answer/id #uuid "ba2da6db-bd61-57eb-b670-5d089274ab2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358841} {:answer/id #uuid "da71a241-5820-5b41-8770-95e613e06e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358842} {:answer/id #uuid "c3851783-37ff-5d63-83af-edc1363edc16" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358843} {:answer/id #uuid "95498863-d2c4-5a40-9649-e023ccb4bca4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358844}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba2da6db-bd61-57eb-b670-5d089274ab2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358841} :answer-field/id #uuid "3b52ee70-6a45-59a8-a321-86ffdfd82696" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358839}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "17a0543f-34f5-5c67-8971-3c0e51750b04" :question/math-academy-id "q-131959" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩$ for $0\\le t<2\\pi ,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi ).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩ \\\\ ⟨3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4t⟩ & =⟨0,-3,4\\pi ⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=-3 \\\\ 4t=4\\pi .\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=\\pi .$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(0,-3,4\\pi )$ is at the tip of $r(\\pi ).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,4⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,4⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3,0,4⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{(-3)^{2}+{0}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{25} \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,-3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,3,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -3 & 0 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-12i-9k \\\\  & =3(-4i-3k) \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =3\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-3)^{2}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{16+9} \\\\  & =3(5) \\\\  & =15\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{{5}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{{5}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358845 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a9f80daa-4180-5dba-a7c1-33c60608f102" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358847} {:answer/id #uuid "e4ba86bc-7907-576d-9fef-c98ae4c8972b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358848} {:answer/id #uuid "402009ae-2d6d-5b28-bb8d-3e5ad61e5c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358849} {:answer/id #uuid "75b51958-c1eb-5e61-95e3-e0f98dfc81e1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358850} {:answer/id #uuid "29fd0d04-e4ea-5d71-8f46-446e91c2e6b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358851}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "402009ae-2d6d-5b28-bb8d-3e5ad61e5c91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358849} :answer-field/id #uuid "a0282fe1-1422-51b7-b372-3e4d4328ba2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358846}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0556bd30-af31-5596-a2e3-09c389614b69" :question/math-academy-id "q-131968" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {e}^{-t}=1 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,-{e}^{-t},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},-{e}^{-0},2⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,-1,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-{e}^{-t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,{e}^{-t},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0},{e}^{-0},0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i+2j+2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+4+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{(\\sqrt{6})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{6\\sqrt{6}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358852 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e638470-8ce9-5e9d-af2f-747328c49416" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358854} {:answer/id #uuid "14967e8b-2741-542b-9821-eeab3806648f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358855} {:answer/id #uuid "333c13fd-c3bd-5dac-97ae-a5a4fbb9e10f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358856} {:answer/id #uuid "c154d580-626e-507f-a824-19c52ccccd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358857} {:answer/id #uuid "7a21af80-abba-5c34-84e9-adc272087d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358858}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c154d580-626e-507f-a824-19c52ccccd9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358857} :answer-field/id #uuid "5f38f90d-3b12-52a6-9ad1-4e6db97597eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358853}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "64fc2a14-fee2-5335-8823-e0d8cf577911" :question/math-academy-id "q-131970" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t=1 \\\\ {e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t=0 \\\\ t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the third equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨{e}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0-{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,1⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-2{e}^{-t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-2{e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡0,\\,-2{e}^{-0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡0,\\,0⟩⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,-2,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-2i-2k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-2)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(-2)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4+0+4} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{(\\sqrt{3})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{3\\sqrt{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358859 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cac0dff-665c-548e-b3f9-48d218f1a7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358861} {:answer/id #uuid "8137a203-e5e1-5af1-ab88-eaf966f2b693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358862} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f314e2-a7dc-54cb-afa6-0562c9488e1d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2{t}^{2}}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358863} {:answer/id #uuid "c56577ee-d483-54ed-b604-c48c5588da0a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358864} {:answer/id #uuid "3436a242-fc1d-5700-bc0b-78658948856f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{t}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0cac0dff-665c-548e-b3f9-48d218f1a7ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358861} :answer-field/id #uuid "3449f4f3-aafd-5ac2-8d55-93ff5d0b8773" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358860}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6b9412b-65ef-57cc-92ea-d6766fc6fcf3" :question/math-academy-id "q-132067" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t-2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,0⟩\\times ⟨-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 0 \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =[(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)]k \\\\  & =[2{cos}^{2}⁡t-3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{sin}^{2}⁡t+3t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+{t}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡t]k \\\\  & =(2({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)+{t}^{2}({sin}^{2}⁡t+{cos}^{2}⁡t))k \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+2)k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖={t}^{2}+2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{{(\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1})}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{t}^{2}+2}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ab4f07c4-6a4f-5760-944f-42f0496cb0b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358868} {:answer/id #uuid "f48e1755-003b-5005-a01e-e9125f99bf0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2t}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358869} {:answer/id #uuid "07b92213-8180-59d7-bbb7-11a54407e889" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358870} {:answer/id #uuid "4a081ba5-cdec-5033-bfc8-6ddcd73394f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358871} {:answer/id #uuid "504fdf52-a521-592a-b704-e51121413bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358872}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "504fdf52-a521-592a-b704-e51121413bf7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358872} :answer-field/id #uuid "79328c8c-0883-5f8e-8cd0-b2d1d76aa674" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358867}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "da86715d-8126-5962-aa89-fa51c40c78dc" :question/math-academy-id "q-165242" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},\\,2{t}^{2},\\,2t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j.$*" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2t,\\,4t,\\,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(2t)}^{2}+{(4t)}^{2}+{(2)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+16{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4(5{t}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-8\\,i+4\\,j,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-8)^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{64+16} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{80} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{(2\\sqrt{5{t}^{2}+1})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{5}}{8\\sqrt{(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{5}}{2(5{t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d4cbc2e-9d33-5798-a347-3ddd07980b9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358875} {:answer/id #uuid "fe228705-a15f-5bab-b95e-033544aaf9ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358876} {:answer/id #uuid "c38538d5-d651-51ff-a392-e5df4c4d4a9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{t}^{2}+1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358877} {:answer/id #uuid "9b45df3c-d62b-5fd7-bdd5-734b80993078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358878} {:answer/id #uuid "d232be54-1563-5e82-b841-0e4a733b29eb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9b45df3c-d62b-5fd7-bdd5-734b80993078" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358878} :answer-field/id #uuid "7460673c-2d72-5d62-ac67-5a952dfd384b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fc6695d3-06a1-5f96-b0f7-737ffb144ac0" :question/math-academy-id "q-165244" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3},\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature of this curve as a function of $t.$ \n\n*Hint: You can use the fact that* ${r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,\\sqrt{2}t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2})}^{2}+{(\\sqrt{2}t)}^{2}+(1)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+2{t}^{2}+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & ={t}^{2}+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that ${t}^{2}+1>0$ for any real value $t.$\n\nSince we are told that\n\n$$\n{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)=-\\sqrt{2}\\,i+2t\\,j-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2}\\,k,\n$$\n\nwe have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}+(-\\sqrt{2}{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+4{t}^{2}+2{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{{({t}^{2}+1)}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120235 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "920d9611-7181-5210-969c-984452d0ea8e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117911} {:answer/id #uuid "d47e86f1-d930-582a-a8a5-1ad492ca8fe6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117912} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f123cb-1b6e-5516-8f83-9ae4f165c4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117913} {:answer/id #uuid "083c3319-bcc0-58ab-a572-803e269dba91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117914} {:answer/id #uuid "61a3cb47-4fd7-510f-bb93-caa965c4b0fb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117915}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2f123cb-1b6e-5516-8f83-9ae4f165c4ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117913} :answer-field/id #uuid "8bed7300-6781-5205-97ba-d0b28838a6b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186117910}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96d2a067-654d-55aa-b5c4-b48e4bab8c2c" :question/math-academy-id "q-34202" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩$ at the point where $t=\\pi .$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(\\pi ),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(\\pi ) & =⟨1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨2,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi ):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi ⟩=⟨0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(\\pi )$ and ${r}^{″}(\\pi )$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi ) & =⟨2,\\,0,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-2\\,k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖ & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=\\pi $ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(\\pi ) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi )\\times {r}^{″}(\\pi )‖}{‖{r}^{′}(\\pi ){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{{2}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358880 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55dfb07c-371c-5bb0-9c93-947330cfd3e6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{1/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358882} {:answer/id #uuid "863fe0d9-93d3-5b7b-9011-f90f4b7fcc85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{18}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358883} {:answer/id #uuid "bda5e74e-d083-56ce-9dc3-328b12f7771f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358884} {:answer/id #uuid "0f7033fe-8d58-5a00-b91d-dab9a8f9a307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358885} {:answer/id #uuid "ea585fbf-a997-58e2-8e9b-ea9ed0591556" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358886}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f7033fe-8d58-5a00-b91d-dab9a8f9a307" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358885} :answer-field/id #uuid "064cad32-5831-57e8-ba64-ffbeb303b597" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358881}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "556293fc-3c7f-5f32-bc07-48e166851c18" :question/math-academy-id "q-34381" :question/problem "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨{t}^{2},{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨2t,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(2t)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨2,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨2t,\\,3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨2,\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2t & 3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 2 & 6t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}2t & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 2 & 6t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=6{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{(t\\sqrt{4+9{t}^{2}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6{t}^{2}}{{t}^{3}(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{t(4+9{t}^{2})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358887 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d598d2b6-445b-51a3-afcd-c6db6a5ea2ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358889} {:answer/id #uuid "af6bd89b-aef9-5651-98b9-18a06731d916" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358890} {:answer/id #uuid "f6a1cb31-4691-54f2-bd28-26dc11691a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358891} {:answer/id #uuid "ac243059-1ee1-51a0-a5b5-3642096abd8c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358892} {:answer/id #uuid "1e238e7f-e27b-555a-96be-1329f9bd0234" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358893}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f6a1cb31-4691-54f2-bd28-26dc11691a37" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358891} :answer-field/id #uuid "e82bdf8a-aea0-5e30-9a74-cf499c8dcf1d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358888}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "42e675d7-c153-56a4-a60e-5f69b95ab1fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-64761" :question/problem "Using the cross product curvature formula, find the curvature of $r(t)=⟨\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩$ at the point where $t=0.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$ and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{0}{\\sqrt{1-{0}^{2}}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨0,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1-{t}^{2}}}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\frac{1}{{(1-{t}^{2})}^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨-\\frac{1}{(1-{0}^{2})^{3/2}},\\,{e}^{0}⟩=⟨-1,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(0)$ and ${r}^{″}(0)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨0,\\,1,0⟩\\times ⟨-1,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{1}{(1)^{3}}=1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358894 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fa5d8ef-02ae-5024-bfe1-202aee9190bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358896} {:answer/id #uuid "96e2c389-0303-5d1d-9e14-5df830b874aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358897} {:answer/id #uuid "cd06b297-72a6-58de-ac7a-3d9ada651f35" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358898} {:answer/id #uuid "83461d3b-e6d9-54bd-aeb2-2fc4b0ba255c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358899} {:answer/id #uuid "bfce075a-c691-59ca-af67-f0cd241ec419" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358900}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96e2c389-0303-5d1d-9e14-5df830b874aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358897} :answer-field/id #uuid "865089c0-2f55-5735-913e-196d4b70d3a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358895}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ea9eaa52-b07b-579c-b027-1db28f380c68" :question/math-academy-id "q-83401" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩$ for $t\\ge 0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(1,0,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(1,0,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨1,0,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨{e}^{t},\\,{t}^{3},\\,2t⟩ & =⟨1,0,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t}=1 \\\\ {t}^{3}=0 \\\\ 2t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=0.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(1,0,0)$ is at the tip of $r(0).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(0),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(0)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,3{t}^{2},\\,2⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(0) & =⟨1,0,2⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+{2}^{2}}=\\sqrt{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(0):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}2)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{e}^{t},\\,6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(0) & =⟨1,0,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =2j \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}}=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=0$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(0) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(0)\\times {r}^{″}(0)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(0){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{5})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{5}}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358901 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "eddc00c0-eac2-5e79-84be-b90cc27eb054" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a117f076-775d-5747-a5cd-6f842baa977a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358903} {:answer/id #uuid "055afff6-82e4-52d8-bc90-e7a97c956b65" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\sqrt{2}}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358904} {:answer/id #uuid "ef3d9bab-704b-5baa-9890-c3dfd09a2f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358905} {:answer/id #uuid "a400555e-21a3-5c83-a2b3-7f3a99baccdf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358906} {:answer/id #uuid "e9f6a2f0-4cbc-589a-9d93-f89626128665" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358907}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ef3d9bab-704b-5baa-9890-c3dfd09a2f47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358905} :answer-field/id #uuid "9ebd2a1b-13cc-5ba4-a03c-4c4816f6a261" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358902}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "36e34b5e-9c3c-5c2f-91a2-6dd8d8382f33" :question/math-academy-id "q-83402" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩$ for $t>0$, use the cross product curvature formula to compute the curvature at the point $P(2,1,0).$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the value of $t$ that corresponds to the point $P(2,1,0)$ on the given curve. We know that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\ ⟨2t,\\,{t}^{2},\\,\\operatorname{ln}⁡t⟩ & =⟨2,1,0⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the respective components gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2t=2 \\\\ {t}^{2}=1 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡t=0.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equality, $t=1.$ This value also satisfies the other two equations. Therefore, $P(2,1,0)$ is at the tip of $r(1).$ \n\nNow, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$, ${r}^{′}(1),$  and $‖{r}^{′}(1)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨2,\\,2t,\\,\\frac{1}{t}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{′}(1) & =⟨2,2,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{{2}^{2}+{2}^{2}+{1}^{2}}=3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find ${r}^{″}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{t})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,\\,2,\\,-\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}⟩ \\\\ {r}^{″}(1) & =⟨0,2,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1) & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-4i+2j+4k \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4)^{2}+{2}^{2}+{4}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16+4+16} \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature at $t=1$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(1) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(1)\\times {r}^{″}(1)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(1){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{{3}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358908 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7246e363-ff25-566e-b86a-571c7c94a623" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358910} {:answer/id #uuid "fd8dd439-a0ac-57a6-b6b8-c5c4b31cf7ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358911} {:answer/id #uuid "80d69cde-422c-5a5b-9d14-f05f4df9dccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358912} {:answer/id #uuid "4f80c751-1b9c-54fb-b67e-702cd3878c4b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{({t}^{2}+1)^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358913} {:answer/id #uuid "3c0dbd08-ed1d-5b81-98d4-4c7ab696c421" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358914}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "80d69cde-422c-5a5b-9d14-f05f4df9dccc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358912} :answer-field/id #uuid "933a210b-69c7-500d-b43f-79703540fcc6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "bd9cfa87-7dff-5a04-a405-35501e7125bc" :question/math-academy-id "q-83439" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by:\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}+(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+2{cos}^{2}⁡t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross-product and its magnitude:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & \\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\ -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & -\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}i-\\sqrt{2}j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{2})}^{2}+{(-\\sqrt{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2+2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{2}{(\\sqrt{2})^{3}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358915 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "80266bb2-9d25-548c-9741-94de26e98805" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358917} {:answer/id #uuid "7f016fca-d7a4-5cf3-9a3d-4e2c9bd8be1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358918} {:answer/id #uuid "38060121-0bf1-5af1-a15e-71cbd58dcaa9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358919} {:answer/id #uuid "7a79e1c0-3fb0-5ec7-a381-8095f28d65c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358920} {:answer/id #uuid "97c36ceb-e02d-5b19-b92a-2847538913c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3t}{4(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358921}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a79e1c0-3fb0-5ec7-a381-8095f28d65c2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358920} :answer-field/id #uuid "027cb776-8049-57af-9ac8-33a225c351f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "08daaac0-fbd4-5500-8a88-44cc20910ea2" :question/math-academy-id "q-86785" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨3,\\,3t,\\,{t}^{3}⟩$ for $t>0,$ use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3})⟩=⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{0}^{2}+(3)^{2}+(3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9(1+{t}^{4})} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨0,\\,3,\\,3{t}^{2}⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,0,\\,6t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 0 & 3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & 6t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}0 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =18t\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|18t|=18t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{18t}{{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})}^{3}}=\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358922 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3b61ddc7-b781-5a25-b499-1ea6f48abf36" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Curve at a General Point"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b70b624f-4e86-53f1-871d-5db6b744ee99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358924} {:answer/id #uuid "1d9de9f0-1f45-50a7-a7f1-9379aaff10d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{{sin}^{2}⁡(t)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358925} {:answer/id #uuid "62b12cce-be61-514e-989b-c0fe8d17d9dd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358926} {:answer/id #uuid "14b70082-d898-5772-ba41-00c5572a75f8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358927} {:answer/id #uuid "2a6962a4-edef-5657-82f7-e0d0ed2a42bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2a6962a4-edef-5657-82f7-e0d0ed2a42bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358928} :answer-field/id #uuid "0eb7d9c3-e2f2-5e38-8f8d-32510fd8d2ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358923}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "04ab73dc-e7b5-5ca6-9a3f-c828499c1554" :question/math-academy-id "q-86799" :question/problem "Given the curve $r(t)=⟨-t,\\,4,\\,1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩$ for $0\\le t\\le \\pi $, use the cross product curvature formula to find the curvature as a function of $t.$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ(t)$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)⟩=⟨-1,\\,0,\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}+(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the second derivative ${r}^{″}(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-1),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)⟩=⟨0,\\,0,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the cross-product:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨-1,0,\\operatorname{cos}⁡t⟩\\times ⟨0,0,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩ \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ -1 & 0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|i-|\\begin{aligned}-1 & \\operatorname{cos}⁡t \\\\ 0 & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}|j+|\\begin{aligned}-1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}|k \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\,j\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we compute the magnitude of the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖ & =|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\operatorname{sin}⁡t\\ge 0$ when $0\\le t\\le \\pi .$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{(1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t))^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186358929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ca07aabe-9fdd-5f28-b39f-1971c3591f86" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Curvature of a Plane Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf4ec583-e157-5fb7-9366-299b67a611ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{9(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358931} {:answer/id #uuid "eaaf97ed-d317-5fa1-97e2-d5e9fe88e466" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358932} {:answer/id #uuid "61910a38-c809-5331-a61d-4006120d4a01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{t}{27(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358933} {:answer/id #uuid "e9992417-8e8b-5a75-874f-3519067c1c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358934} {:answer/id #uuid "2c03cbcb-7002-5e12-a5a0-6301c6593e52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6t}{\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358935}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9992417-8e8b-5a75-874f-3519067c1c3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358934} :answer-field/id #uuid "3f665fad-ce17-5821-b8e1-2f7882ffd131" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186358930}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "20720610-3b29-5e15-904f-53aa6331528d" :question/math-academy-id "q-86800" :question/problem "What is the curvature of the curve $r(t)=⟨3t,1-{t}^{3}⟩$, for $t>0?$" :question/worked-solution "The curvature $κ$ is given by\n\n$$\nκ(t)=\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{{‖{r}^{′}(t)‖}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate ${r}^{′}(t)$ and $‖{r}^{′}(t)‖:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-{t}^{3})⟩=⟨3,-3{t}^{2}⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{{3}^{2}+(-3{t}^{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9+9{t}^{4}} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the second derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{″}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3),\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(-3{t}^{2})⟩=⟨0,-6t⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we compute the cross product by considering ${r}^{′}(t)$ and ${r}^{″}(t)$ as $3$-dimensional vectors with zero $z$-components:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t) & =⟨3,\\,-3{t}^{2},\\,0⟩\\times ⟨0,\\,-6t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 3 & -3{t}^{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6t & 0\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}3 & -3{t}^{2} \\\\ 0 & -6t\\end{aligned}\\right|\\,k \\\\  & =-18t\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the magnitude of the cross product is\n\n$$\n‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖=|-18t|=18t.\n$$\n\nNotice that since $t>0$, we have $|t|=t.$\n\nTherefore, the curvature is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}κ(t) & =\\frac{‖{r}^{′}(t)\\times {r}^{″}(t)‖}{‖{r}^{′}(t){‖}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18t}{(3\\sqrt{1+{t}^{4}})^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{18t}{27(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{3(1+{t}^{4})^{3/2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
